x86_64-linux  The VTT and the construction vtables, which clang emits and cxx1 does not: `_ZTT2D1`, `_ZTT2D2`, `_ZTT3Dia`, `_ZTC3Dia0_2D1`, `_ZTC3Dia8_2D2`, and the `_ZTI3Dia`/`_ZTS3Dia` pair with them. Nothing wants them until a virtual function is called from inside a base constructor, which is the one thing a construction vtable exists to make right, and this compiler refuses that shape rather than emitting a table it has not measured. The feature's own remainder, recorded when the virtual base landed; every name cxx1 does emit agrees with clang.
arm64-darwin  The same seven, with Mach-O's leading underscore. Same reason, same remainder.
x86_64-windows  Two names cxx1 emits and clang does not: `??_DD1@@QEAAXXZ` and `??_DD2@@QEAAXXZ`, the vbase destructors of the two middle classes. That is Itanium's D1 under its Microsoft name - it destroys the class's own part and then the virtual bases - and this case never destroys a D1 or a D2 as a *complete* object, so clang emits neither. cxx1 emits one beside every destructor of a class with a virtual base, the way it emits D1 beside every D2, and an unused COMDAT definition costs a fold. **The names this case is about all agree**: `??_8D1@@7B@`, `??_8D2@@7B@`, `??_8Dia@@7BD1@@@` and `??_8Dia@@7BD2@@@`, checked against cl itself by names-vs-cl on the box.
